## Why This Is Asked

Test practical tracing and end-to-end visibility across CNPA during retries and regional outages, ensuring the correlation persists across services and layers.

## Key Concepts

- Correlation IDs / distributed tracing across HTTP, DB, Kafka, Redis
- Middleware propagation, headers, and context propagation
- Retry semantics, idempotent writes, and rollback strategies
- Observability: logs, metrics, traces, dashboards

## Code Example

```javascript
// Express middleware to ensure correlation ID propagation
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const id = req.headers['x-correlation-id'] || uuidv4();
  req.correlationId = id;
  res.setHeader('X-Correlation-Id', id);
  next();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify the correlation ID is preserved through PostgreSQL writes and Kafka messages?
- How would you test this under retries and partial outages, including automated end-to-end tests?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes your ability to diagnose multi-service latency issues under multi-tenant load, with realistic trade-offs between per-tenant isolation, backpressure, and end-to-end tracing.

## Key Concepts

- Distributed tracing and context propagation across HTTP, Redis, DB, Kafka, and sink
- Per-tenant rate budgets and backpressure
- Tail latency analysis and query plan instrumentation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: OpenTelemetry span around HTTP handler and downstream calls
const span = tracer.startSpan("http.request", { parent: ctx });
try {
  // propagate span context to Redis and Kafka clients
  await redisClient.call("LIMIT", { tenantId: tenant }, { span });
  await kafkaProducer.send({ key: tenant, value: payload, span });
} finally {
  span.end();
}
```

